      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dunbar sprang up in 1856 when John Dunbar and Thomas H. combined four neighboring farm plots into a new settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dunbarnebraska.com/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally called Wilson, the town was renamed Dunbar in 1856 to honor its oldest resident, John Dunbar.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dunbarnebraska.com/history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The arrival of the Midland Pacific and Burlington‑Missouri River railroads in 1869 turned Dunbar into a vital rail link.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dunbarnebraska.com/history",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Dunbar grew into a large freighting depot, handling goods heading west from Nebraska City.",
